Everything the language is, one section per idea.

## [Programs and modules]($section.id('programs'))

A program starts at `fn main()` in its entry file, a plain function taking nothing and returning nothing. An entry is one file, and everything it imports is part of the program.

A module is one file. `import helper` reaches `helper.phi` beside the entry, `import std.io` reaches the standard library, and the last name in the path is the name the file uses. A declaration crosses files only when it is marked `pub`.

```
import std.io

fn main() {
    io.print("Hello, world!\n")
}
```

The prelude declares `none`, `true`, `false`, `bool`, and `str` once, visible in every file. None of them is built into the compiler: `bool` is `type bool = true | false`, a union like any other, and `str` is `[]u8`. A file's own declaration still wins over the prelude's.

## [Lines and comments]($section.id('lines'))

A statement ends at the end of its line. A `,` or `;` also ends one, so short statements may share a line. A line that opens with `.` or `..` continues the line above, which is how a long chain breaks cleanly.

```
// a comment runs to the end of the line
/// a doc comment belongs to the declaration below it
//! a file doc comment describes the whole file
```

A line that opens with `-`, `&`, `~`, `(`, or `[` after a bare line break could continue the line above or start a new statement, so the compiler refuses the ambiguity and asks for a `,` or a rewrite.

## [Bindings]($section.id('bindings'))

`let` binds a value that cannot change, `var` one that can. An annotation seals the type, so `let n: u64 = 2` is a `u64` everywhere it goes. A bare literal stays untyped until it lands somewhere, and takes any type its value fits.

```
let n: u64 = 2
let small = 7        // untyped, fits wherever 7 fits
var count: u32 = 0
count += 1
_ = small            // '_ =' drops a value on purpose
```

A `var` needs a type before it can vary, so `var x = 1` is refused and `var x: i64 = 1` is not. Names cannot shadow: a local may not reuse the name of another local, a parameter, a type parameter, or a declaration in its file. `_` is not a name, and stands only in `_ = expression`.

Top-level bindings are `let`, and hold a value the compiler can work out before anything runs: literals, names of constants, operators, and parentheses.

## [Numbers]($section.id('numbers'))

The integer types are `i8` through `i64` and `u8` through `u64`, with `f32` and `f64` beside them. Literals are decimal, hex `0x`, octal `0o`, or binary `0b`, with `_` between digits and `.` and `e` for floats. Constants fold in 128 bits, exactly.

A narrower number lands on a wider one with nothing written, because every value of the narrower type is already a value of the wider. Signedness may change where no value is lost, and an `f32` is exactly an `f64`. The two ends of an operator meet the same way, so a `u32` counter reaches a `u64` length with no conversion between them. Nothing lossy converts on its own.

```
let big: u64 = 10
let small: u32 = 3
let sum = big + small              // u64
let back = @int_cast[u32](big) or 0
```

`@int_cast(n)` is the conversion that can lose a value, so it answers `T | none` and `or` says what happens when it does not fit. It converts to what it lands on, and takes `[T]` where nothing lands it.

## [Unions]($section.id('unions'))

One keyword declares every type.

```
type Timeout                       // a unit type, whose only value is its name
type Reply = u32 | Timeout | none  // a union
type Port = u16                    // an alias
type Point = {                     // a struct
    x: u32
    y: u32
}
```

`A | B` is a union type: members are distinct types, order is part of the type, and a member that is itself a union flattens in place, so aliases compose. `Reply` above is exactly `u32 | Timeout | none`, and writing `u32 | Reply` would be refused for repeating `u32`.

A value enters a union by membership, unwrapped, and comes back out only through a branch that proves the member.

```
fn fetch(attempts: u32) Reply {
    if attempts > 3 {
        return Timeout
    }
    return 200
}
```

A condition is a union asking whether it holds its first member, so `if`, `and`, and `or` all branch on that one question. `bool` is `true | false`, so a comparison already answers it.

## [Narrowing]($section.id('narrowing'))

`e is T` tests which member a union holds and narrows the tested name to it: in both branches, across `and`, and past a branch that leaves. `is not` swaps the two sides.

```
fn use_it(r: u32 | none) u32 {
    if r is u32 {
        return r        // r is a u32 here
    }
    return 0            // and here it is none
}

fn guarded(r: u32 | none) u32 {
    r is u32 or return 0
    return r            // the guard proved it
}

fn positive(r: u32 | none) u32 {
    if r is u32 and r > 0 {
        return r        // the 'and' carried the proof
    }
    return 0
}
```

Narrowing follows `let` bindings and parameters, never a `var`, because a `var` can be rewritten between the proof and the use.

## [or]($section.id('or'))

`e or f` is the first member of `e`, or else `f`. On a `bool` it stays logical or.

```
fn substitute(flag: bool) u32 {
    return lookup(flag) or 8080
}

fn propagate(flag: bool) u32 | Timeout {
    let value = fetch_one(flag) or return
    return value + 1
}

fn handle(flag: bool) u32 {
    let value = fetch_one(flag) or e {
        report(e)
        return 0
    }
    return value
}
```

`or 8080` substitutes a value. A bare `or return` sends the rest of the union up, unchanged, so a function that answers `u32 | Timeout` propagates a `Timeout` with two words. `or e { ... }` handles: the block runs with `e` bound to the rest of the union, and either produces the first member or leaves.

## [match]($section.id('match'))

`match` splits a union into one arm per member, exhaustive by counting. An arm may label several members with `A | B`, and `else` is the rest as a type rather than a blind default, so the arm's body knows exactly what it holds.

```
fn describe(r: u32 | Timeout | none) u32 {
    return match r {
        u32 => r
        Timeout => 100
        none => 0
    }
}

fn grouped(r: u32 | Timeout | none) u32 {
    return match r {
        u32 => r
        Timeout | none => 0
    }
}
```

The scrutinee narrows per arm, the way `is` narrows a branch, so fields and methods of the proven member are reachable inside the arm. An arm that leaves narrows what follows the match.

```
fn after(flag: bool) u32 {
    let r = lookup(flag)
    match r {
        none => return 0
        u32 => {}
    }
    return r + 1        // r is a u32 past the match
}
```

## [Loops]($section.id('loops'))

`loop { }` runs until a `break`, `loop cond { }` while the condition holds, and `loop i in 0..n { }` counts a range: the name is a `let` for the pass, both ends are read once before the first, and the counter takes the type of the ends, `u64` where neither says.

```
fn sum(xs: []u32) u32 {
    var total: u32 = 0
    loop i in 0..xs.len {
        total += xs[i]
    }
    return total
}
```

A label reaches an outer loop, and `break v` with `else` makes a loop an expression: the `else` says what the loop is worth when it ends on its own.

```
fn press(tries: u32) bool {
    var attempt: u32 = 0
    let found: bool = loop attempt < tries {
        if probe() is u32 {
            break true
        }
        attempt += 1
    } else {
        false
    }
    return found
}

fn scan() u32 {
    var hits: u32 = 0
    outer: loop i in 0..10 {
        loop j in 0..10 {
            if i * j > 20 {
                break :outer
            }
            hits += 1
        }
    }
    return hits
}
```

## [Arrays and views]($section.id('arrays-and-views'))

`[N]T` is an array, N values of one type laid out end to end, written `[1, 2, 3]`. The length lives in the type, so it costs no memory and answers as `a.len`. An index past the end is refused before anything runs, or checked before the memory is touched.

`[]T` is a view of elements it does not own, two words wide: the address as `.ptr` and the count as `.len`. `[]var T` also writes through, and fits wherever a `[]T` is asked. A view holds no elements, so a type may hold a view of itself, and `[]T | none` costs no more than the view alone.

`a[x..y]` is the single bridge from storage to a view, and `a..` runs to the base's own length. What the view may write is what the place it came from allowed.

```
fn views() {
    var buffer: [64]u8 = @splat(0)
    let view: []u8 = buffer[0..16]
    let rest = view[4..]
    _ = rest
}
```

A string is bytes and a character is a number, both written as constants that fit where their values fit: `"text/plain"` returned as `[]u8` is bytes the program owns, `let magic: [2]u8 = "hi"` is storage with its length checked, and `s[i] == 'a'` compares two numbers. Escapes decode at compile time, and a literal never crosses a line.

A constant that lands on a view gets bytes the program owns, so `[2, 3, 5, 7]` fits a `[]u32`. It never fits a `[]var T`, because the program's own bytes are read-only.

## [Pointers]($section.id('pointers'))

`*T` reads through, `*var T` also writes through and fits where a `*T` is asked. `&x` takes an address, and `p.*` reads what a pointer points at.

```
fn bump(n: *var i64) {
    n.* += 1
}

fn same(a: *u32, b: *u32) bool {
    return a == b
}
```

`==` and `!=` compare two pointers by the address each one is, and what may be written through a pointer is not part of that. `*T | none` is one word, with `none` as the zero no valid pointer holds, so an optional pointer costs nothing over the pointer.

## [Structs and methods]($section.id('structs'))

A struct holds fields and functions. A struct literal names the type it builds, `Point.{ x: 1, y: 2 }`, or leaves the name out where what it lands on says it. Every field must be present, and there are no defaults.

A function whose first parameter takes the struct is called through a value, and the receiver arrives as `T`, `*T`, or `*var T`, whichever the declaration asked for. A function without one is called through the type.

```
type Counter = {
    hits: i64

    fn total(self: Counter) i64 {
        return self.hits
    }

    fn bump(self: *var Counter, by: i64) {
        self.hits += by
    }

    fn zero() Counter {
        return .{ hits: 0 }
    }
}

fn drive() i64 {
    var counter = Counter.zero()
    counter.bump(2)
    return counter.total()
}
```

A method that writes through its receiver needs the receiver to be a `var`. A method without `pub` is private to its file, the way every other declaration is.

## [Generics]($section.id('generics'))

Generics are written `[T]` and instantiate wherever a type is written. Identity is the instantiation, so `Box[i64]` twice, or once through an alias, is one type.

```
type Pair[K, V] = {
    key: K
    value: V

    pub fn swap_value(self: *var Pair[K, V], value: V) V {
        let old = self.value
        self.value = value
        return old
    }
}

type Maybe[T] = T | none
```

A call usually needs no type arguments: what it feeds pins them, through `T`, `*T`, `[]T`, and `[N]T` alike, and the annotation on what the call feeds reaches a type argument no parameter mentions.

```
fn pairs() i64 {
    var pair = Pair[bool, i64].{ key: true, value: 41 }
    let old = pair.swap_value(42)
    let missing: Maybe[u32] = none
    _ = missing
    return old + pair.value
}

fn aligned() u64 {
    let a: u64 = mem.align_up(11, 8)   // the annotation decides T
    return a
}
```

## [defer]($section.id('defer'))

`defer` runs a statement on the way out of its scope, innermost first, on every path out. A `defer` cannot leave again, so no `return` or `break` is written inside one.

```
fn greet() {
    defer io.print("second\n")
    io.print("first\n")
}
```

## [Operators]($section.id('operators'))

Tightest first. Suffixes bind tighter than any operator: a call `f(x)`, an index or type argument `a[i]`, a field `p.x`, and a read through a pointer `p.*`.

|Level|Operators|
|-----|---------|
|prefix|`-`, `not`, `~`, `&`|
|9|`*`, `/`, `%`|
|8|`+`, `-`|
|7|`<<`, `>>`|
|6|`&`|
|5|`^`|
|4|`\|`|
|3|`==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, `is`|
|2|`and`|
|1|`or`|

Comparisons do not chain: `a < b < c` is refused, and is written as two comparisons joined with `and`. Compound assignment exists for every arithmetic and bitwise operator, `+=` through `>>=`.

`==` and `!=` compare numbers, and pointers by address. Everything they refuse says what answers instead: `is` for a union, `std.mem.eql` for a view, a written comparison for a struct.

## [Builtins]($section.id('builtins'))

An operation the compiler performs itself is written `@name`, and stands only in a call.

|Builtin|Answers|
|-------|-------|
|`@size_of[T]()`|The bytes a value of `T` occupies, a constant.|
|`@align_of[T]()`|The alignment `T` requires, a constant.|
|`@min_int[T]()`, `@max_int[T]()`|The edges of an integer type, exact.|
|`@int_cast(n)`|The number where the type holds it, `none` where it does not.|
|`@splat(v)`|An array whose every element is `v`, sized by where it lands.|
|`@trap()`|Stops the program where it stands.|

The answers that are constants arrive untyped, so `let n: u32 = @size_of[Point]()` needs no conversion.

## [What stops a program]($section.id('traps'))

There is no unwinding and nothing to catch. Overflow, division by zero, a shift past the width, an index past the end, a range that leaves its base, and a violated `std.debug.assert` stop the program at the instruction that failed.

Everything the compiler can settle before anything runs is refused at compile time instead, with the same rules: a constant index past the end, a constant that overflows, a range whose ends cross.
